  "query_preview": "..\n\nA stable airmass in place over southeast Michigan for now, as dew \npts started the day in the 50s. A MCS over the western Great Lakes \ntracking across southern Lake Michigan will attempt to transport \nmoisture/instability across the border by early this evening. \nSurface dew pts are currently lowers 70s degrees over northern \nIndiana. Sufficiently strong (40 knots) southwest low level jet \nearly this evening to transport instability/solid negative showalter \nindices into the south half of the CWA. The stronger wind fields and \nlocalized stronger 0-6 KM bulk shear will support a chance of severe \nstorms, especially toward the southern Michigan border this evening \nwhere the sharp instability gradient will reside. Machine learning \nsuggests all severe hazards are in play, including an isolated \ntornado as 0-1 KM bulk shear increases above 20 knots. Despite mid \nlevel lapse rates looking average, MLcapes aoa 1500 J/kg suggests \nlarge hail threat, which is in addition to the main hazard, damaging \nwinds. However, if abrupt southeast development occurs with the \nupstream MCS and it dives into northern Indiana (a distinct \npossibility), our severe threat will be diminished as the moisture \ntransport fails to materialize. Regardless, the deep moisture plume \n(PW up to 2 inches) looks to be fold over and descend south into the \nOhio Valley overnight as 850 MB winds veer more westerly. Thus, \nchances for training and significant flooding rainfall seems to be \nlow locally, with much higher chances over the northern Ohio Valley. \n\nThere is fairly good model agreement regarding subsidence and dry \nconditions for most of Monday, before some late-day attempt to \ndestabilize as a subtle shortwave rides along in the west-northwest \nupper level flow established over the Great Lakes region. Once \nagain, the corridor along and south of I-94 will have to be watched \nfor possible severe storms during Monday evening/night. \n\nThe close proximity of the moisture axis and substantial instability \nover the Ohio Valley on Tuesday will also need to be watched closely \nas the strength of upstream shortwave tracking through Midwest is \nassessed. NBM pops suggest sufficient moisture transport returning \ninto the southern part of the state will supports likely showers and \nthunderstorms near the border, along with a low severe threat. \n\nThe jet stream/strong westerlies will remain across the northern \nGreat Lakes through bulk of the work week and will become enhanced \non Thursday as upper-level ridge builds over the southeastern United \nStates to close out the work week. Predominately confluent upper \nlevel flow supports dry conditions and near-normal temperatures.",
